<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Serbian Vojvodina</h1><p class="st-meta">1848 CE – 1849 CE · capital Sremski Karlovci</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Self-proclaimed Serbian autonomous province in southern Hungary during the revolution of 1848, whose name lives on in today's Vojvodina.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> asserted its independence in spring 1848, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> of southern Hungary feared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyarisation">Magyarisation</a>. A national assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sremski-karlovci">Sremski Karlovci</a> in May 1848 proclaimed the Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrmia">Srem</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baranya">Baranya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/backa">Bačka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a>, elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josif-rajacic">Josif Rajačić</a> patriarch and Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stevan-supljikac">Stevan Šupljikac</a> voivode, and set up a Main Board led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorde-stratimirovic">Đorđe Stratimirović</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a> was a wartime administration rather than a settled state. Its forces, joined by volunteers from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-serbia">Principality of Serbia</a>, fought Hungarian revolutionary armies in bitter ethnic warfare across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/backa">Bačka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serb</a> leaders allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> court against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>, hoping for autonomy as a reward.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary's</a> defeat in 1849, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/voivodeship-of-serbia-and-banat-of-temeschwar">Voivodeship of Serbia and Banat of Temeschwar</a>, an Austrian crown land run by German-speaking officials rather than by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>. The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a>, however, became established for the region.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/serbian-vojvodina.jpg" alt="Serbian Vojvodina" width="320" height="206"><figcaption>Image: Felipe Fidelis Tobias · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Serbian_Vojvodina.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1848 CE – 1849 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sremski Karlovci</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Josif Rajačić, Stevan Šupljikac, Đorđe Stratimirović</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Serbian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Serbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>, <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Serbian Voivodeship (1848), Srpska Vojvodina</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1848&amp;focus=serbian-vojvodina" aria-label="Serbian Vojvodina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5154 582 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Serbian Vojvodina lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5474 838L5398 749M5474 838L5503 821"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5398 749h0M5503 821h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5474 838h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5474 838h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sremski Karlovci. <a href="/atlas?year=1848&amp;focus=serbian-vojvodina">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
